<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    GOLDEN-VECTOR WIRE TESTS — byte-exact, deliberately NOT round-trip.

    WHY NOT ROUND-TRIP. A sender never parses its own packet, and both serializers are
    index-walkers with no tags. The most likely damaging refactor is therefore one that
    moves a block in `Write` AND in `TryRead` together — exactly what a "tidy the
    serializer by grouping the ifs per feature" pass does. Writer and reader stay mutually
    consistent, so `Write(x); TryRead(...) == x` still passes while every peer on the old
    build is corrupted. Only expected BYTES catch that.
    (.planning/refactor/REVIEW-Net-Rig.md §2.1 and §W5.)

    WHY SOURCE-LINKED AND NOT `InternalsVisibleTo`. Adding
    `[assembly: InternalsVisibleTo("GloomhavenVR.Tests")]` to the mod would put an assembly
    attribute in the shipped DLL, and `ilspycmd -p` emits assembly attributes — so the
    refactor guard would report a diff for a test-only change. Compiling the five wire
    files into this assembly instead makes them internal TO THIS ASSEMBLY, needs no
    attribute, and leaves the shipped DLL untouched. Guard diff: empty.

    NOT IN GloomhavenVR.sln, ON PURPOSE. `refactor-guard.sh` builds the solution and greps
    the output for "error"; a test project in the solution puts test compilation on the
    critical path of the mod's safety net. Run it with `scripts/wire-tests.sh`.

    NOT SHIPPED. `scripts/package-release.sh` copies named artifacts out of
    src/*/bin — it never looks at tests/.

    UNITY TYPES ARE THE REAL ONES. `Mathf`/`Vector3`/`Quaternion` come from the game's own
    UnityEngine.CoreModule.dll and run fine outside Unity (they are managed code for
    everything these serializers touch). A hand-written shim was rejected: `Mathf.RoundToInt`
    is banker's rounding (RoundToInt(0.5f) == 0, RoundToInt(1.5f) == 2), which a shim would
    almost certainly have got wrong — and it sits directly on the quantization path.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <TargetFramework>net8.0</TargetFramework>
    <AssemblyName>GloomhavenVR.WireTests</AssemblyName>
    <RootNamespace>GloomhavenVR.WireTests</RootNamespace>
    <AllowUnsafeBlocks>true</AllowUnsafeBlocks>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <!--
      CS1574 off HERE ONLY, and by construction rather than by retreat. This project compiles a
      deliberately chosen SUBSET of the mod's sources (see the Compile items below and the reason
      written at each). A doc comment inside one of them may reference EnvSoundBank, ConfigCatalog,
      Flight, HandStyle and so on — types that exist in the plugin and are simply not part of this
      compilation. The reference is correct; it just cannot resolve in a unit that does not contain
      the type. The plugin's own build checks all of them, as an error.
    -->
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);CS1574</NoWarn>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
    <!-- The mod's own analyzers/publicizer are irrelevant here and would need the game refs. -->
    <EnableDefaultCompileItems>false</EnableDefaultCompileItems>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!-- The wire files, compiled into the test assembly verbatim. If one of these is split or
       moved during the refactor, this list is what fails first — which is intended. -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <Compile Include="$(RepoRoot)src\GloomhavenVR\Net\NetProtocol.cs" Link="wire\NetProtocol.cs" />
    <Compile Include="$(RepoRoot)src\GloomhavenVR\Net\Avatar\AvatarState.cs" Link="wire\AvatarState.cs" />
    <Compile Include="$(RepoRoot)src\GloomhavenVR\Net\Avatar\AvatarSerializer.cs" Link="wire\AvatarSerializer.cs" />
    <Compile Include="$(RepoRoot)src\GloomhavenVR\Net\PresenceState.cs" Link="wire\PresenceState.cs" />
    <!-- Record 45's SHAPE and fold. Split out of UseBarSlotSymbol.cs precisely so that
         PresenceState can be compiled here: the other half names CActiveBonus, CItem and
         Sprite, and this project references UnityEngine.CoreModule and nothing else. -->
    <Compile Include="$(RepoRoot)src\GloomhavenVR\Net\UseBarSlotIdentity.cs" Link="wire\UseBarSlotIdentity.cs" />
    <Compile Include="$(RepoRoot)src\GloomhavenVR\Net\NetPacket.cs" Link="wire\NetPacket.cs" />
    <!-- Record 28's PAGING (2026-08-09). It belongs on this list for the same reason the
         serializers do: it is a wire format with a sender and a receiver that never meet, and its
         convergence property ("a peer that joins at T holds the complete tuning by T + pageCount
         packets") is not observable from either end alone. Written free of Unity, config and the
         game model precisely so it could be linked in here and driven byte by byte. -->
    <Compile Include="$(RepoRoot)src\GloomhavenVR\Net\Board\BoardTunePages.cs" Link="wire\BoardTunePages.cs" />
    <!-- The card-FX endpoint CODEC (2026-09-06, user item 8b). Two anchors are packed into one
         byte's two nibbles and unpacked by a receiver that may be a DIFFERENT BUILD from the
         sender: NetCardFx.Clamp is the only thing standing between an id this build has never
         heard of and a card arcing to a place that does not exist. That is a sender-and-receiver
         property no single end can observe, which is exactly this project's definition of a wire
         format. It links cleanly because it is free of Unity and the game model — its only
         dependency is the VRLog shim beside this list. -->
    <Compile Include="$(RepoRoot)src\GloomhavenVR\Net\NetCardFx.cs" Link="wire\NetCardFx.cs" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!-- Not a wire file, same reasoning: the preloader's relaunch command is built at the one moment
       the mod deliberately kills the game, so a quoting defect there is undiscoverable in place.
       It was written free of BepInEx and process access precisely so it could be linked in here. -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <Compile Include="$(RepoRoot)src\GloomhavenVR.Preload\RelaunchCommand.cs" Link="preload\RelaunchCommand.cs" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!-- Not a wire file either, and here for the same reason as RelaunchCommand: it decides
       something that is only ever observed by FEEL, one ◀ / ▶ press at a time, from inside a
       headset. A step a hundred times too small does not fail, does not log and does not look
       wrong — it produces a dial the player reports as having no effect. Three rounds of exactly
       that reached the user (see ConfigStepVectors.cs). ConfigSteps.cs is free of Unity, BepInEx
       and the game model precisely so it could be linked in here and driven key by key. -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <Compile Include="$(RepoRoot)src\GloomhavenVR\WorldUI\Options\ConfigSteps.cs" Link="worldui\ConfigSteps.cs" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!-- Not a wire file either, and here for the ConfigSteps reason exactly: it arbitrates one
       thumbstick between menu scrolling and turning, and its verdict is only ever observed by
       FEEL, from inside a headset. A latch that never releases does not throw, does not log and
       does not look wrong — it produces a player who cannot turn (user report 2026-08-11). Its
       release edge and its two exits are unreachable from anywhere else in this repo, so
       ScrollTurnGate.cs was written free of Unity (beyond Mathf), of config and of the hand model
       precisely so it could be linked in here and driven frame by frame. -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <Compile Include="$(RepoRoot)src\GloomhavenVR\Rig\ScrollTurnGate.cs" Link="rig\ScrollTurnGate.cs" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!-- Not a wire file either, and here for the ScrollTurnGate reason exactly: it arbitrates the
       SAME thumbstick, between turning and the vertical lift added on the user's request of
       2026-08-15, and its verdict is only ever observed by FEEL. One comparison too loose and the
       world yaws every time the player tries to rise; one too tight and the control silently
       refuses for anyone whose thumb is not perfectly vertical, which is indistinguishable from a
       broken button. Its "45 degrees is always a pure turn" and "a snap flick can never also lift"
       properties are claims the German menu caption makes to the player, and neither is reachable
       from anywhere else in this repo. LiftWedge.cs was written free of Unity (beyond Mathf), of
       config and of the hand model precisely so it could be linked in here. -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <Compile Include="$(RepoRoot)src\GloomhavenVR\Rig\LiftWedge.cs" Link="rig\LiftWedge.cs" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!-- Not a wire file either, and the sharpest case on this list. It schedules the bursts inside
       two synthesized one-shots, and its failure mode is not a wrong sound but NO FRAME AT ALL:
       ModBuild 145 drove the creak from a `while` whose geometric step summed SHORT of the window
       it tested against, so the loop never ended and the game froze on the loading screen — no
       exception, no warning, and the last line in Player.log written a few statements earlier by
       something else entirely. A test that HANGS is the loudest possible regression signal for
       that, which is why the schedule was pulled out of EnvSound.Bank.cs (AudioClip, AudioSettings
       — the whole Unity audio module) into a file free of everything but Mathf. -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <Compile Include="$(RepoRoot)src\GloomhavenVR\Core\Sound\EnvSoundSchedule.cs" Link="core\EnvSoundSchedule.cs" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!-- Not wire files either, and here for the EnvSoundSchedule reason at its sharpest. Spatial
       voice chat is observed ONLY by ear, from inside a headset, with a SECOND PERSON on the other
       end of a microphone — the least reproducible test in this repo, and the user cannot run it at
       all for a week. A rolloff 12 dB too steep does not throw, does not log and does not look
       wrong; it produces a teammate who "cuts out" and a report nobody can localise. An indicator
       whose threshold sits where speech level actually lives does not fail either; it strobes,
       which is the "super nervig" verdict this project already collected once, on the ice sound.
       Both are decided by arithmetic, so VoiceCurve.cs was written free of Unity (beyond Mathf), of
       config and of the game model, and VoiceIcon.cs free of everything at all — it does not even
       call Math.Sqrt — precisely so both could be linked in here and driven metre by metre and
       texel by texel. VoiceIcon being linked is also what lets the icon renders in .planning/voice/
       be produced BY THE SHIPPED RASTERISER rather than by a lookalike. -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <Compile Include="$(RepoRoot)src\GloomhavenVR\Voice\VoiceCurve.cs" Link="voice\VoiceCurve.cs" />
    <Compile Include="$(RepoRoot)src\GloomhavenVR\Voice\VoiceIcon.cs" Link="voice\VoiceIcon.cs" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!-- Not a wire file either, and here for the ConfigSteps reason at its sharpest. It decides how
       dark a haunt apparition is drawn and how it materialises, and both are observed ONLY by feel,
       from inside a headset, one photograph per round. Through ModBuild 152 the darkening
       multiplier was written into a material colour property the game's character shader ignores,
       so its four constants were re-fitted FOUR TIMES against photographs in which the lever did
       nothing — four shipped builds, four "immer noch viel zu hell" reports, and nothing in this
       repository that could have noticed. The mapping and the materialise window were pulled out of
       HauntFigures.Clone.cs / .Events.cs (Addressables, Animator, the whole clone factory) into a
       file free of everything but Mathf precisely so they could be linked in here and driven
       instant by instant. -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <Compile Include="$(RepoRoot)src\GloomhavenVR\Core\Haunt\HauntFigures.Math.cs" Link="core\HauntFigures.Math.cs" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!-- Not a wire file, and here for the ConfigSteps/ScrollTurnGate reason: it decides the
       interaction volume of the two-handed resize gesture, and that verdict is observed ONLY by
       feel, from inside a headset. Its predecessor was a FIXED 0.5 m ceiling that stopped growing
       while the figure did, so past a certain stretch it excluded the mini's own renderers and the
       capture test silently fell back to the centre distance — a player who could enlarge a figure
       and then not reach it again (user report 2026-08-15, and the second time that report was
       filed). Nothing threw, nothing warned, and the mod could not have noticed. The arithmetic was
       kept out of FigureStretch.cs (VRHand, Renderer bounds, the config, the grabbable) into a file
       free of everything but Mathf precisely so it could be linked in here and driven size by size. -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <Compile Include="$(RepoRoot)src\GloomhavenVR\Board\FigureGrab\FigureStretchMath.cs" Link="board\FigureStretchMath.cs" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!-- Not a wire file either, and here because its failure mode is a RELATIONSHIP between two
       things a screenshot only ever shows one of. HeldPoseMirror is the single definition of "the
       other hand" for a held miniature and a held map item alike; get one of its three sign flips
       wrong and BOTH hands still look plausible on their own, while the pair is wrong — which in a
       headset reads only as "das ist verdreht" (user, the 2026-09-05 handedness round). The file was written free of
       HandSide, of every config entry and of every Unity component — plain bools, floats,
       Vector3 and Quaternion — precisely so the left/right pair could be driven here against the
       reflection it is supposed to be, rather than against a table of remembered angles. -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <Compile Include="$(RepoRoot)src\GloomhavenVR\Board\FigureGrab\HeldPoseMirror.cs" Link="board\HeldPoseMirror.cs" />
    <Compile Include="HeldPoseMirrorVectors.cs" />
  </ItemGroup>

